--------------------------------------------
- Reference Types and Reference Assignment -
--------------------------------------------

In Java, all non-primitive types are reference types. You will recall that Java has only eight primitive types. 
Any data type that is not one of the following eight will be a reference type.

-> Java Primitive Types (Chart) 
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| boolean | a logical type with valid values of true and false				                  |
| byte	  | an 8 bit integer type with a range of -128 to +127					              |
| short   | a 16 bit integer type with a range of -32,768 to +32,767                          |
| int     | a 32 bit integer type with a range of -2,147,483,648 to +2,147,483,647            |
| long    | a 64 bit integer type with a range of roughly (+/-) 9 * 10 ^ 18                   |
| float   | a 32 bit floating point type with reduced range and precision                     |
| double  | a 64 bit floating point type with a large range and high precision                |
| char    | a 16 bit integer type that can store any unicode character                        |
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

	-> Non-primitive types are reference types in Java... <-

As you know, all variables in Java must be declared, and the variable declaration must specify the datatype of the 
variable. A variable of a non-primitive type must store a reference to an object of that type, or the special null 
reference. A variable of type Scanner always stores a reference to a Scanner object, or it stores a null reference. 
A null reference means that the variable does not refer to any object at this time.

-> Value Types vs. Reference Types

This is very different behavior from a value type variable. A value type variable will always store a value of that 
type. For example a variable of type int will always store an int value - the value may be positive, negative, or zero, 
but there will be an int value stored in an int variable at all times. On the other hand, a reference type variable can 
store a reference to an object of the variable's type, but it can also store a null reference. So, a reference type 
variable is either null (referring to no object at this time), or it is referring to an object of the variable's type. 
So, while a value type variable can never be empty, a reference type variable can be null (which is essentially empty).

	-> Reference type variables: either store an object reference or they store a null reference. <-

->  Null Reference vs. Object Reference

If we declare a variable named input of type Scanner, then that input variable will either store an object reference or 
a null reference. The declared variable will store a null reference (by default initialization), if we do not 
explicitly initialize the variable to an object reference. While an object reference is a reference to some actual 
object in memory, the null reference is a reference to nothing.

	Scanner input; // Scanner variable storing a default null reference 

We can also use the Java keyword null to explicitly store a null reference in a reference type variable. 
The keyword null is the Java literal for a null reference.

	Scanner input = null; // Scanner variable storing a null reference

When we assign a value to a reference variable (like this input variable) we will be replacing the reference that it 
currently stores with a new reference. Below, we are instantiating a new Scanner object and storing a reference to this new object in our input variable.

	input = new Scanner(System.in); // replace null ref. With object ref.  

To see the difference between a reference variable that currently stores a null reference, and that same variable 
storing a reference to an object of the variable's data type, consider the following code.

	Scanner input = null; // Scanner variable storing a null reference
	System.out.print("Enter your name: ");
	String name = input.nextLine();

---------------------------- Code Editor 13.18.1: null reference Exception Example 1 --------------------------------

import java.util.Scanner;
 
public class Main {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Scanner input = null; // variable storing null reference
 
    System.out.print("Enter your name: ");
    String name = input.nextLine();
 
    System.out.println("Hello " + name + "!");
  }
}

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

You should get the following runtime error message if you run the code above.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	Exception in thread "main" java.lang.NullPointerException
   		at Driver.main(Driver.java:8)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This is a very common runtime error for many Java programmers. But fear not, the solution to this error is always 
simple. This error always means the same thing - that on the specified line of code (line 8 in this case) you are 
asking a nothing to do something. But, a nothing cannot do something.

	null is nothing.

The null reference is a reference to nothing. Since there is a null reference stored in the input variable, the 
variable refers to nothing. So, on line 8 we are asking this nothing to do something input.nextLine(), and Java 
responds with an error (an exception). The solution is to store an actual object reference in the variable before we 
ask it to do something. This usually means that we need to instantiate an object of this type and assign it to the 
variable, which we often do (by explicit initialization) when we declare the variable.

	Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in);

Try the revised code below to see that it now works; because we now have a reference to an actual Scanner object stored 
in our input variable.

----------------------- Code Editor 13.18.2: null reference Exception Example 2 ----------------------------------------

import java.util.Scanner;
 
public class Main {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in);
 
    System.out.print("Enter your name: ");
    String name = input.nextLine();
 
    System.out.println("Hello " + name + "!");
  }
}

----------------------------------------------
Input : Enter your name : Bob Smith
Ouput : Hello Bob Smith!
