-> Reference Assignment

Recall that a class definition creates a new data type. The following class definition creates a new data type named 
Bunny.

	class Bunny {
	   public int age = 0;
	   public String name = "";

  	   public void hop() {
		 System.out.println(name + " hops around.");
   	   }

	   public void eat(String food) {
      		 System.out.println(name + " eats the " + food + ".");
	   }

           public void sleep() {
	       System.out.println(name + " takes a nap.");
	   }
	}

Given the above class definition, consider the following code.

Bunny flopsy;
Bunny cottontail;

flopsy = new Bunny();
flopsy.name = "Flopsy";
flopsy.age = 8;

cottontail = flopsy; // !!!
cottontail.name = "Cottontail";
cottontail.age = 5;

System.out.println(flopsy.name);

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

You may have been quite surprised to see that flopsy.name is "Cottontail". How did this happen? We explicitly stored 
"Flopsy" in the name variable of the Bunny object that the flopsy variable refers to (flopsy.name = "Flopsy";). And, we 
stored "Cottontail" in the name variable of the Bunny object that the cottontail variable refers to 
(cottontail.name = "Cottontail";).

